The potential vulnerability is related to an HTTP Header Injection vulnerability. This vulnerability occurs because the function appears to directly incorporate user-supplied input (`infoContent`) into HTTP response headers without any validation or sanitization. If malicious or improperly formatted input is provided, it could allow attackers to inject arbitrary headers or manipulate the HTTP response, potentially leading to security issues such as response splitting, cache poisoning, or other forms of exploitation. The lack of input validation or encoding is the root cause of this vulnerability.

